From: aiueo52 <aiueo52@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 20:45:34 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 031/105] hc: one-launch combine (gate + apply in a single
 Triton kernel), SGLANG_HC_COMBINE_FUSED=1

GatedResidual.combine currently launches the sgl-kernel pair hc_combine_gate +
hc_combine_apply.  At 96 boundaries per decode step that is 192 launches; the W16
trace puts the pair at ~3.5 us for 16 rows and ~3.1 us for 1 row while the row only
moves ~1 MB, i.e. it is launch- and parallelism-bound, not bandwidth-bound.

hc_combine_fused_triton does both phases in one launch.  The row is cut into NPROG
contiguous power-of-two chunks and program (m, p) owns chunk p for both phases:
phase 1 accumulates that chunk's contribution to all HC gate dots into a persistent
fp32 partials buffer, a grid barrier publishes them, phase 2 reduces per branch,
forms the gates and streams the chunk out.  Reading the normed row once for all HC
branches (the reference gate kernel reads it once per branch) also drops ~1 MB of L2
traffic per verify-width call.

NPROG is chosen per row count so rows*NPROG stays under the SM count, which is what
makes the grid barrier safe: every CTA is resident before the barrier, a graph replay
serialises the stream, and PDL only releases a successor once this grid is fully
launched.  The wrapper refuses (and combine() falls back to the two-kernel path)
whenever no chunking fits.  The last CTA out restores both counters to 0 so a graph
replay starts clean.

Numerics: fp32 throughout with a bf16 round at the store, as in the CUDA pair, but
the gate dot is reduced in a different order, so the contract is "within 1 bf16 ulp"
rather than bit-identical -- the fp32 gate moves by ~1e-7 relative and that only
flips an output bit when the fp32 result sits that close to a bf16 rounding boundary.
test/srt/layers/test_hc_combine_fused.py asserts max ulp <= 1 across rows 1..16,
prints the measured differing-element fraction, checks the result against an fp64
reference so a systematic error cannot hide in the allowance, and covers the counter
reset and a CUDA-graph replay.

Default off; bench/glue_e/bench_hc_combine.py compares the two under CUPTI.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
